SU(2) extended finite square-boundary insertion exact audit

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research probe_su2_square_boundary_insertion_extended
OMP_NUM_THREADS=26 OMP_DYNAMIC=false \
  ./probe_su2_square_boundary_insertion_extended 8 4

OUTPUT
SU2_SQUARE_BOUNDARY_INSERTION_EXTENDED maximum_level=16 maximum_coefficient=4 threads=26 profiles=2441392 admissible_squares=926559 insertion_checks=7138109 boundary_failures=0 first_failure=(-1,-1,-1,0) first_profile=[] first_square=[] first_output=[] result=NO_SQUARE_BOUNDARY_INSERTION_FAILURE

METHOD
For every half-level 1<=K<=8, the audit exhausts every nonzero
character-positive profile p with coefficients in {0,1,2,3,4}.  It
forms the exact finite fusion square d=p^2, retains precisely the
log-concave squares satisfying every simple-current boundary current,
and applies N_Q twice for every nontrivial irreducible factor
1<=Q<=K.  Every resulting boundary current is evaluated with exact
arbitrary-precision integers.

The thread count is a runtime choice made from contemporaneous CPU load
and available RAM; it is not a mathematical bound or a source-level
limit.  The zero failure count is bounded evidence for direct
square-boundary insertion, not an unbounded proof.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror -fopenmp \
  probe_su2_square_boundary_insertion_extended.cpp \
  -o probe_su2_square_boundary_insertion_extended

